# Native Linux installation realm: recovery design v1

This document is a newly authored, read-only recovery proposal from actual restored D6/old18/NEW28 materials. It is not the lost native22 proposal and does not borrow its hash, approval, test or source identity. Code implementation, compiler/install/setup, namespace/cgroup creation, seed download, privileged bootstrap and native installation effects performed here: zero. All five installation slots remain NOT_RUN. The user authorized necessary installation on disposable repository CI; local privilege, user machines and production are outside this task.

## Actual source obligations
D6 is c60f9667fa8431e27fabbc497c19b4abfef5b965. Durable old18 consumer source installs via the original package/check commands; its historical135 PASS is component-only. NEW28 complete-source admission and native bootstrap are different finite families and cannot share a guessed28 cap. Future native scope is explicitly22 paths (19 additions, three modifications), its union with old18 is37 and its union with NEW28 is47; actual overlap/counts must be frozen from real bytes before qualification. This new proposal has no implementation scope approval or native proof.

## Protected launcher and actual child creation
A disposable CI bootstrap step compiles the fixed reviewed C source with that job's already provisioned native compiler. It records exact source tree, argv, compiler/linker/libc/ELF interpreter hashes and binary hash. A fixed, reviewed sudo install step copies the binary to a newly exclusive root-owned directory outside the user-writable checkout, owner0, fixed group200000 reserved for the coordinator (not the ordinary runner account), mode4750; parent directory0700 is unsuitable for user execution, so directory0710 with only reserved coordinator group200000 traversal is proposed. The directory contains only reviewed binary and no writable loader search locations. Actual mount nosuid/noexec policy, final inode/hash/mode and root-owned dynamic interpreter/dependencies are inspected by native FD/stat/read fences before launch. CI sudo bootstrap is not the retained installer supervisor and does not return a PID to adopt.

The Python coordinator directly Popen-executes this exact protected helper once with closed environment, fixed argv and inherited private socketpair plus seed/source/input FDs. It retains its real Popen child and acquires pidfd while the child is held behind a setup pipe before effects. Unexpected death before pidfd ownership is a sticky setup failure. The helper validates actual ruid equals reserved coordinator hostUID200000 and real GID200000, euid/suid/fsuid0, dumpable0, no attacker-controlled supplementary groups, and exact private FD roles. no_new_privs/nosuid preventing elevation causes refusal. No caller path/env/READY PID, /proc scan, sudo-monitor adoption, SO_PEERCRED dictionary or UID0 report creates a child owner. The helper clears LD_* and GitHub/Actions credentials entirely, closes every FD beyond the declared fixed set, and never execs a shell.

BLOCKED authorization prerequisite: fixed ruid, protected binary, source/hash/Selection FDs, nonce or a private caller-created socket establish identities/transport only; any process with that UID could reproduce them. They do not establish the actual canonical CIInvocation. A reviewed trusted native bootstrap must itself directly create and retain the exact coordinator child/wait/pidfd and a one-use private activation endpoint inherited only by that child; coordinator must still directly create the installation helper. Bootstrap keeps its native link and original controls held and validates that same concrete creation/activation relation; neither a serialized token/UID/PID message nor sudo-monitor adoption can issue the lease. Coordinator/activation FD retention, cross-process exact-holder binding and inability of a foreign process to duplicate or inject require a separately reviewed concrete owner implementation and native negatives. A pre-exec PR_SET_DUMPABLE(0) followed by ordinary Python exec is insufficient: execve can reset dumpable to1, and a Python entry-time reset leaves a loader/import/activation-read window. The concrete candidate loading branch instead reserves a separate host coordinator UID200000, distinct from the caller UID and every guest mapping100000..165535, exclusively for the actually created child. Trusted native bootstrap clears supplementary groups, switches all real/effective/saved/fs UID/GID credentials to exclusively reserved hostUID200000/GID200000 behind the actual creation gate, and loads only root-owned fixed Python interpreter/stdlib/coordinator source with closed environment/import paths; it retains the child pidfd/wait and activation endpoint through the entire exec/import phase. Ordinary host caller and all guest IDs must fail native ptrace/process_vm_readv, descriptor-duplication and activation-injection attempts during that whole phase. Actual namespace/LSM/proc policy, UID reservation, interpreter/libc/import identity and native loading proof are UNKNOWN. HostUID200000 or root-owned files alone do not issue the lease: the trusted issuer must already hold the actual canonical task-authorized CIInvocation owner before it creates this child, refuse arbitrary restarts and duplicate use under a concrete live one-use native owner, and create no activation when that independent issuer is absent. This issuer is still missing; changing the UID or writing a root-owned serialized grant cannot supply it. That issuer is missing today: setuid4750 alone is not enough to activate the helper, and the production route remains denied. The protected bootstrap is task-authorized disposable-CI setup, not claimed available on current runners. If installing/protecting/loading this fixed executable cannot be established, the slot stops before seed/package effects. Installation ownership remains the directly created retained helper, not the bootstrap sudo process.

## Pristine Ubuntu seed and normal package compatibility
Choose signed Ubuntu archive bootstrap for jammy on Ubuntu22.04 and noble on Ubuntu24.04, not a copy of the runner root. A fixed native seed-acquisition operation uses a pinned debootstrap binary/source and pinned Ubuntu archive keyring (actual hashes must be obtained and sealed in that job). It performs signature-enforced first-stage/bootstrap downloads against fixed archive host/suite/architecture; no no-check-gpg, certificate bypass, caller URL or unsigned fallback. Debootstrap supports suite/mirror/keyring and separate foreign/second-stage phases; its manual documents these mechanisms. [Primary documentation](https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/debootstrap/debootstrap.8.en.html).

The first-stage extraction must be a reviewed native archive decoder with no escaping/hardlink/device/symlink traversal or arbitrary host installation scripts. Second-stage package instructions run only after the namespace/cgroup before-exec gate described below. Freeze exact signed InRelease/Packages/deb hashes, all package/version dependencies, seed manifest and complete native root identity; actual values are pending, not placeholders issuing approval. Seed apt database and normal runtime packages include sudo, Python3, xvfb/xauth/dbus, desktop-file-utils, ldd/binutils and the actual DEB Depends closure from the selected candidate. Package installation preserves the complete original argv from restored install.py325: `/usr/bin/sudo /usr/bin/apt-get install -y GUEST_PACKAGE_PATH`, executed by guest1000 and elevated by actual guest sudo to guest0; the only declared translation is the verified package FD copy to its owned guest path. Original package120-second command bound and same guest apt database remain. The guest /usr/bin/sudo inode/owner0/mode4755, PAM libraries/config, caller account1000/group policy and exact root-owned sudoers rule permitting the fixed apt command are frozen seed inputs, never a blanket caller command permission. Actual sudo setgroups/setuid/exec transition is a required native positive. The old host workflow dependency-install step becomes the reviewed signed realm seed dependency preparation; it must not continue installing dependencies into the host and claim those are guest runtime compatibility. Download apt dependencies into owned caches during the original realm lease and make the actual install offline by containing network, without replacing apt by extraction or altering its original argv. A missing dependency or changed signed repository snapshot fails the slot.

A source export contains the exact selected source bytes/modes plus a freshly constructed private native Git object database/HEAD/refs needed by original source checks, copied only from the exact verified canonical history. Its new config has only reviewed core/object-format settings; no source host config, credential helper, remote URL, hooks, alternates, user HOME, tokens or arbitrary host paths is bound into the realm. Native Git graph and full modes/blobs are freshly verified before the read-only export; an export omitting required .git history would fail original source checks and is not this design. Package copy is made from the retained verified FD into the native owned root. Source mapping uses guest /source and candidate mapping /inputs; these are explicit native operation translations, not original host argv identity claims. Original source verification and installed-file paths must resolve in that same guest realm. Freeze actual guest Ubuntu os-release/architecture/package/libc/runtime identities; matching release labels alone are not compatibility proof.

The entire seed/bootstrap/installation/check/retirement lifetime shares the original900-second maximum. This proposal does not allocate a new lease before/after setup. If signed seed/dependencies cannot be obtained within that bound, retain failure; any later scheduling/producer proposal is a separate reviewed contract.

## Creation-time containment, exact mappings and credentials
The root helper obtains a fresh exclusive cgroup-v2 parent directory FD, freezes its actual dev/inode/mount relation and keeps that parent empty. It creates two sibling leaf cgroups: monitor (trusted PID1 reaper) and workload (package/GUI descendants). It creates the trusted, still-gated reaper into that owned parent using clone3 with CLONE_PIDFD|CLONE_INTO_CGROUP|CLONE_NEWUSER|CLONE_NEWPID|CLONE_NEWNS|CLONE_NEWNET|CLONE_NEWIPC|CLONE_NEWUTS|CLONE_NEWCGROUP, exit_signal SIGCHLD. clone3's native creation return/pidfd and cgroup FD bind the child before it can run a package. ENOSYS/EINVAL/EPERM or incompatible controller policy causes refusal; no late attach fallback. The reaper blocks on a private one-use gate. The new cgroup namespace is rooted at this owned parent, so both monitor and workload are within its namespace. During trusted setup, before any seed/package instruction or untrusted fork, host supervisor moves this same native held reaper into monitor via the owned monitor cgroup.procs and reads back its exact actual membership and unchanged pidfd relation. The owned parent is then empty; the gated package/GUI child still must be created directly into workload using CLONE_INTO_CGROUP, with no post-exec attach. Moving trusted setup before the first package is an explicitly declared phase, not package containment claimed retroactively. clone3 and creation-time cgroup support are documented kernel interfaces, not proof a runner allows them. [Primary manual](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/clone.2.html).

Proposed exact map is guest IDs0..65535 to a newly reserved host range100000..165535 for both uid_map/gid_map. This is a concrete proposed range, not a proven free range or permission; native CI bootstrap checks collisions, files, processes and runner policy and refuses unless exclusively reserved. Real host root retains CAP_SETUID/CAP_SETGID while writing child maps. Use the documented privileged gid-map branch with setgroups allow; do not irreversibly write deny, because the preserved guest sudo transition requires real group setup. Clear inherited supplementary groups natively before map/credential changes and verify the only intended mapped groups on each guest transition; owned guest accounts and root-owned sudo/PAM group policy are configured before code execution. Any host policy requiring deny is an incompatibility and blocks this original-sudo route, never a reason to remove sudo or fake group changes. Guest root never maps to host0 or the host coordinator UID. Native source/input mounts remain read-only; writable guest root seed file ownership is translated into this mapping by the actual opened filesystem owner. User-namespace capabilities govern that namespace rather than granting powers in an ancestor. [Primary manual](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/user_namespaces.7.html).

The trusted reaper performs private mount propagation, installs owned root, pivots into it, detaches old root and closes every unrelated ancestor namespace/root/setup FD before package execution. Trusted reaper retains only its internally owned scoped parent/common-ancestor and workload cgroup spawn FDs until complete native retirement; those FDs are never inherited by package/GUI children. Retention of these scoped spawn FDs is explicit and does not leave a generic host ancestor handle. Native guest /proc is mounted for its own PID namespace; /run and /tmp are private, /sys/cgroup and host sysfs are absent, /dev is an explicitly minimal native filesystem (null, zero, random, urandom, tty as required), no host block devices, Docker/container sockets, service-manager bus or host network. Fresh network namespace has only loopback for owned DBus/GUI tests. For each workload, reaper directly creates a gated child with clone3 CLONE_PIDFD|CLONE_INTO_CGROUP into the workload leaf using its private native cgroup FD, then closes that FD in the workload child before gate release and never passes it to descendants. The reaper retains native wait ownership outside workload; unknown child setup retains the whole realm. No cgroup FD/mount is passed to package descendants, so migration control cannot be reached through an inherited handle or path. Exact control-passing/close ordering is a required native test; a post-exec attach is rejected. An opened cgroup FD alone does not authorize CLONE_INTO_CGROUP. The host supervisor must establish exact limited delegation of workload cgroup.procs and the scoped common-ancestor migration check to the reaper mapped host UID100000, with every outside ancestor/sibling inaccessible; read back actual ownership/mode/namespace-root/nsdelegate policy and succeed through the real native creation primitive before releasing any workload gate. Scoped parent-root cgroup namespace makes monitor/workload reachable under that root; a namespace rooted only at monitor would not satisfy this contract. The delegation/migration/write permissions and runner policy remain UNKNOWN until native CI proves them. Guest namespace CAP_SYS_ADMIN or a retained FD is never substituted for host cgroup permission; denied delegation/placement refuses before package effects.

Package-root effective/permitted/bounding caps are exactly CHOWN,DAC_OVERRIDE,FOWNER,FSETID,KILL,SETGID,SETUID,SETPCAP,NET_BIND_SERVICE,SYS_CHROOT,SETFCAP in the guest user namespace; inheritable/ambient0. Drop SYS_ADMIN,SYS_PTRACE,MKNOD,NET_ADMIN,NET_RAW,BPF,PERFMON,CHECKPOINT_RESTORE,SYS_MODULE,SYS_RAWIO and all unlisted bits from the bounding set before the package fork. Native capget/securebits/readback verifies actual sets and unmapped ancestry. Guest package root therefore cannot recover ancestor powers by exec. Compatibility with the specific DEB maintainer scripts is a fresh native test, not assumed. [Capability definitions](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/capabilities.7.html).

Do not set blanket no_new_privs or nosuid on the whole realm before the original nonroot-to-sudo apt transition: that would silently defeat the original installer. Instead, realm capability bounding and guest-only UID mapping constrain setuid elevation. GUI/check operations switch real/effective/saved/fs UID and GID to guest1000, groups empty, all caps0; only after the package-install stage do GUI operations set no_new_privs=1. Check original GUI-root rejection and actual ELF installed bytes before executing it. Reaper/host helper are not ptraceable by package or GUI; helper/reaper dumpable0 and PID/credential separation must be measured. The trusted PID1 reaper installs no package-triggerable termination handler; ordinary package signals do not instruct cleanup or retirement, and actual private host-supervisor channel is its only operation entry. Package-root attempts to ptrace/kill/modify the reaper remain explicit native negatives, not assumptions from a UID label. Unexpected service-manager endpoint or uncontained process is a sticky failure.

## Fixed IPC and operation authority
One SOCK_SEQPACKET socketpair is created by the coordinator before directly creating the helper. Native frame header is32 bytes: magic8, version u16=1, tag u16, payload_length u32(max65504, so total header+payload frame≤65536), monotonically increasing sequence u64, invocation_nonce u64 generated internally. Reject MSG_TRUNC, ancillary FDs, duplicate/out-of-order/unknown tags, flags, extra bytes or wrong nonce. Request tags are PREPARE=1, INSTALL_DEB=2, CHECK_DEB=3, CHECK_APPIMAGE=4, START_GUI=5, CANCEL=6, RETIRE=7; no generic EXEC/path-command operation exists. Native source owns every fixed argv/env/root mapping. Responses describe phase/result/hash/length only; no serialized owner/grant/callback is accepted.

PREPARE carries immutable bounded Selection/source tree/input digest plus absolute monotonic original deadline; these are expectations checked against inherited owned source/input FDs, not authority. Native coordinator uses its actual retained Stage/ReceivedInputs/invocation/controller objects before every request and after every native fence. Responses cannot renew controls. Helper constructs its actual native realm/family once; each request requires current expected sequence, actual source/input inode/bytes and unchanged cancellation/deadline. Original command budgets remain package120, startup20/60, native600, controller900, always min(original remaining lease, command bound).

CANCEL is sticky and can only strengthen native cancellation. Parent EOF/death or protocol fault triggers cleanup, not adoption/retry. Shared timerfd/eventfd/poll loops use the same CLOCK_MONOTONIC domain and original absolute deadline; no per-phase900 reset. stdout/stderr have per-stream2MiB and64KiB read chunks; private SOCK_SEQPACKET IPC uses one native65536-byte recvmsg buffer for each complete frame, with header32/payload≤65504 and MSG_TRUNC always fatal, drain continuously and reject truncation/overflow. No credential, host environment or arbitrary source path enters guest argv/env.

## Held ownership and retirement order
The coordinator retains exact child/pidfd/IPC plus Stage/source/inputs/invocation/controller until the native family is proven retired. Host helper retains root/reaper pidfd/wait/cgroup/root/mount/seed/source/input owners. Reaper stays outside each killable package group, acts as subreaper and owns every actual fork; it observes direct leader exits with waitid(P_PIDFD,WEXITED|WNOWAIT) before any final release. WNOWAIT preserves native waitable child identity; ECHILD is meaningful only for the actual reaper whose complete closed realm is proven. [Primary wait manual](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/waitid.2.html).

On normal completion: source/input/control fence; stop new requests; drain every process/I/O endpoint; reaper reaps all adopted children and reaches native ECHILD while retaining its own held root identity; supervisor verifies contained family absence, same cgroup/mount/namespace identities and no outside endpoints; signal orderly reaper exit; supervisor waitid-WNOWAIT its actual child; all I/O EOF and worker joins; final source/input/control fence; reap reaper; checked guest mounts/root/source/input/cgroup teardown; helper exits; coordinator observes actual helper exit with WNOWAIT, verifies private EOF and final own fences, reaps same native child, verifies ECHILD, and closes IPC/pidfd last. Native checked close/unmount failure or an unavailable fence keeps the entire owner strongly retained and marks failure.

On cancellation/deadline/fault: native cgroup.kill targets only workload, never the monitor/reaper leaf or their shared parent. pidfd signals may terminate the actual owned workload family, but cgroup populated0 is only supplemental observation, not retirement. Drain/reap/ECHILD/EOF/fences and checked closure remain required. Never signal a numeric PGID after leader reaping, never accept JSON DONE, supervisor exit0, empty cgroup or UID0 as permission to release Python inputs. The kernel cgroup documentation describes containment/migration/kill state, which does not prove private I/O, native child wait or external service effects by itself. [Primary kernel documentation](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html).

